WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:04.019
Now, before we dive into actually creating our sequence,

2
00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:07.980
there are a few core principles or structures

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.779
that we need to understand as it

4
00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:12.939
relates to translating traditional film

5
00:00:12.939 --> 00:00:17.980
terminology to game development or at least a game engine.

6
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.379
So some of the engine terminology

7
00:00:19.379 --> 00:00:21.620
that we've used, which you probably know about,

8
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.179
are things like static and skeletal meshes.

9
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:26.980
We have actors, pawns, characters.

10
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.579
We have worlds, maps, levels.

11
00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:33.259
Level sequences, control rig, which is a animation feature

12
00:00:33.259 --> 00:00:35.939
that we have available in the engine, volumes.

13
00:00:35.939 --> 00:00:38.979
In fact, we will talk about using trigger volumes when

14
00:00:38.979 --> 00:00:41.740
we do our shot creation process,

15
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.820
blueprints, as well as source control.

16
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.920
Some of our sequence or terminology

17
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.200
that's important to understand is scene assemblies.

18
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.280
So this is going to be as we bring things together

19
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.439
collectively.

20
00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:55.719
We have performance takes.

21
00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.479
We have sets, layouts, and staging,

22
00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:01.399
which will be very similar to our world maps and levels,

23
00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:03.759
but we're going to be using that terminology slightly

24
00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:07.579
different as we're doing our assembly process.

25
00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:11.420
Level sequences and shot sequences, as well as just

26
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.069
shots.

27
00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:14.909
In this particular slide, on the left,

28
00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:17.909
we have more traditional film terminology.

29
00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:20.109
And with that, we have things like a film,

30
00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:22.459
like a feature or short film.

31
00:01:22.459 --> 00:01:25.939
We have acts, so various different major blocks

32
00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:29.180
within our film storytelling process.

33
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.840
We have sequences, scenes, shots, and takes.

34
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.239
And on the right, we have the terminology

35
00:01:36.239 --> 00:01:37.920
that we traditionally use when it

36
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.840
comes to establishing these same things with Unreal.

37
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.450
So we have things like film or a master sequence.

38
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.450
For our act that we would call in film,

39
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.810
we call that more or less just a level sequence.

40
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.129
And then you'll see we have a level sequence

41
00:01:53.129 --> 00:01:55.810
within the actual sequence, the scene itself.

42
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.049
Again, keep in mind that a sequence is really

43
00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.049
just a container.

44
00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:03.530
It's how we use it that changes the way that it operates

45
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.700
and it functions within the engine.

46
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.139
And then, of course, after our level sequence,

47
00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.699
we have shots and we have takes.

48
00:02:11.699 --> 00:02:13.219
Now, something to keep in mind here

49
00:02:13.219 --> 00:02:16.699
is that even though this terminology is something

50
00:02:16.699 --> 00:02:19.659
that's often widely adopted by the industry,

51
00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:21.900
it doesn't necessarily mean that you have to use this

52
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.060
verbatim.

53
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.939
The most important thing when it comes to establishing

54
00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:28.659
Sequencer or your Sequencer workflow

55
00:02:28.699 --> 00:02:32.419
is make sure that you have a system that's both scalable

56
00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:36.460
and simple enough that the average person can understand

57
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.500
its core structure, how to use it, et cetera.

58
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.180
The last thing you want to do is get towards the end

59
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.539
of production and realize you have content spread

60
00:02:43.539 --> 00:02:46.030
everywhere within your project.

61
00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.590
So again, on this slide, this is really more

62
00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.150
just guideposts to help you when it comes

63
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.030
to establishing your workflow with Sequencer

64
00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.150
and how to best organize it based off

65
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.509
of the traditional principles of film.

66
00:02:57.509 --> 00:02:59.830
Now, there are a few plugins that are often used

67
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.030
in game development.

68
00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.990
Now, one major caveat is all of the plugins

69
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.469
that we have here on the screen,

70
00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:08.990
you don't necessarily have to have these plugins enabled

71
00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:12.189
if you're going to be working within game development.

72
00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:16.150
As an example, you may not have any motion capture suits

73
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:21.139
or using the AR kit from Apple to do, like,

74
00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:22.819
for example, facial capture.

75
00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:25.699
So you could disable the Apple AR kit and Live Link.

76
00:03:25.699 --> 00:03:28.139
However, if you are using that workflow,

77
00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:30.020
you're going to want to have those plugins enabled.

78
00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.180
And you can see that there are a series

79
00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.340
of other different plugins that translate

80
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.259
to allowing you to create those real-time sequences

81
00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:41.060
or to be able to do motion capture, right?

82
00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.139
Things that are often used in game development

83
00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:48.180
as it relates to creating cinematics, in-game cut scenes,

84
00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.699
or anything where Sequencer would come into play.
